Final Implementation Instructions

First, inspect the relevant existing code and understand how the systems interact.

Then implement the changes directly within the current architecture.

Reuse existing controls, shared styling, input pipelines, and stable identifiers wherever practical.

Do not create a parallel movement system or duplicate input logic unnecessarily.

Do not modify unrelated character mechanics, attack definitions, physics tuning, or the visual identity of the game.

Prioritize correct gameplay behavior, precise visual consistency, multi-touch reliability, and backward compatibility.

When complete, provide:

1. A summary of the changes made.
2. The relevant files modified.
3. The new explicit input mappings for each supported device.
4. The automated test results.
5. Any visual verification performed.
6. Any remaining limitations.

The desired outcome is simple: Run only runs; Mouvement has its own intentional controls; both mobile layouts have independent Left and Right Mouvement buttons; the arrows are centered; and Jump and Extra Attack look identical in style and size to the rest of ALVA’s action buttons.
